infographic compare-binary-horizontal-underline-text-vs
data
  title Goroutine vs OS thread: the three-orders-of-magnitude gap
  desc Same concurrency, wildly different cost — why 10k goroutines is a Tuesday
  compares
    - label Goroutine — scheduled by the Go runtime
      children
        - label Stack starts at ~2 KB
          desc Grows and shrinks on demand; overflow copies the stack to a bigger segment
        - label Switch costs tens of ns
          desc User-space register swap — no kernel involved
        - label 100k of them ~200 MB of stacks
          desc Fine in one process — 10,000 goroutines is a Tuesday
        - label Invisible to the OS
          desc top and thread counts show nothing; only the runtime knows the count
    - label OS thread — scheduled by the kernel
      children
        - label Stack reserves 8 MB up front
          desc Virtual default on Linux (ulimit -s), reserved whether used or not
        - label Switch costs 1-2 µs
          desc Kernel context switch plus cache pollution on every block or wake
        - label 10,000 of them is an incident
          desc Megabytes of stack each — count bounded by memory and the scheduler
        - label Visible to OS tools
          desc top and thread counts report them directly
theme
  palette #6366f1 #f43f5e
